      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Yes, Alani Nu still has a protein powder, but you will not find it on alaninu.com any more. Alani Nu Whey Protein is a whey isolate and hydrolysate blend with 23 g of protein and 110 calories per scoop in Fruity Cereal, per the label, and it now sells mainly through Amazon. This review covers the label, testing, cost, flavors and the shakes and bars Alani sells instead.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Alani Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Alani Nu Whey Protein is a decent, lean whey isolate blend that has quietly become hard to find. Per the Fruity Cereal label at the time of writing, one scoop gives 23 g of protein, 110 calories and 1 g of sugar from whey protein isolate and whey protein hydrolysate, sweetened with sucralose and acesulfame potassium. Alani Nu no longer lists the powder on alaninu.com, where its protein range is now 30 g ready-to-drink shakes and protein bars, but Amazon.com still sells the Fruity Cereal tub at $44.99, about $1.63 per 25 g of protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you want a low-calorie, isolate-based whey with a sweet cereal flavor, you can find it in stock at a fair price, and you do not compete in drug-tested sport. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you need NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, you avoid artificial sweeteners, or you want a brand that will reliably keep your flavor on the shelf. For tested isolates that are easier to buy, see our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> rankings.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Alani Nu Whey, Fruity Cereal, per the label</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">23 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per 30.9 g scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">110</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sugar (0 g added)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$1.63</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g protein (Amazon)</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Alani Nu Whey and the Best Amazon Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            The one Alani Nu whey flavor still sold by Amazon itself, plus three cereal- and dessert-flavored whey powders to buy if Alani's tub is out of stock or you want a testing claim. Prices are the Amazon prices we saw on November 17, 2026, and they move often.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="does-alani-have-protein-powder" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Does Alani Have Protein Powder Right Now?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Alani Nu does have a protein powder, Alani Nu Whey Protein, but it is no longer sold on the brand's own US store. When we checked alaninu.com on November 17, 2026, the product catalog listed energy drinks, pre-workouts, BCAAs, creatine, greens, capsules, three 30g Protein Shake flavors and four protein bar flavors. There was no whey protein powder in the catalog, in the product sitemap or in the site search, and the old product addresses return a not-found page.
          </p>

          <p>
            The powder has not vanished from retail, though. Amazon.com itself sells and ships Alani Nu Whey Protein in Fruity Cereal, 30 servings, at $44.99 when we checked, and specialty retailers such as Vitacost still carry listings for it. Other flavors have been dropping away: The Vitamin Shoppe marks its Munchies whey listing as discontinued, and Canadian retailer Well.ca shows Confetti Cake, Blueberry Muffin, Lemon Meringue, Peanut Butter Brownie and Frosted Flurry as discontinued. Alani Nu has not published a statement we could find explaining whether the whole powder line is being retired, so treat what is left as remaining stock unless the brand says otherwise.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some context helps. Alani Nu was founded in 2018 by fitness influencer Katy Hearn, per the brand's Amazon listing, and built its name on energy drinks and colorful pre-workouts. Celsius Holdings <a href="https://ir.celsiusholdingsinc.com/news/news-details/2025/Celsius-Holdings-Completes-Acquisition-of-Alani-Nu/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">completed its acquisition of Alani Nu on April 1, 2025</a>, in a deal the company valued at $1.8 billion. Since then the alaninu.com catalog has leaned hard into drinks. We are not going to guess at the reasons for dropping the powder, but the practical effect for buyers is clear: if you want Alani protein in powder form, Amazon is the most dependable place to look, and flavor choice is thin.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you searched for Alani Nu protein because a friend loves the 30g protein shakes, note that those are a different product. The shakes are a milk protein drink built on milk protein concentrate and calcium caseinate, not the whey isolate powder. We cover them, and the bars, in their own section below.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Alani protein product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Where we found it</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Status (Nov 17, 2026)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whey Protein, Fruity Cereal (powder)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Amazon.com, Vitacost</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sold by Amazon.com; not on alaninu.com</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whey Protein, Munchies (powder)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The Vitamin Shoppe</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Marked discontinued</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whey Protein, other flavors</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Well.ca (Canada)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Several marked discontinued</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">30g Protein Shake (ready to drink)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">alaninu.com, Amazon, stores</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Current: Cookies and Cream, Munchies, Fruity Cereal</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein Bar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">alaninu.com, Amazon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Current: four flavors on alaninu.com</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in Alani Nu Whey Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Alani Nu Whey Protein is a blend of whey protein isolate and whey protein hydrolysate, with no whey concentrate, per the label and Alani's Amazon listing. That puts it in the isolate category rather than the cheaper concentrate blends many mainstream tubs use. Isolate is filtered to remove most of the fat and lactose found in milk, and hydrolysate is whey that has been partly broken down into smaller peptides. Alani also says the formula contains no proprietary blends.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Fruity Cereal numbers, per <a href="https://www.vitacost.com/products/alani-nu-whey-protein-fruity-cereal-2-lb-0-7-oz-927-g-152889" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Vitacost's copy of the Fruity Cereal label</a> at the time of writing, are: a 30.9 g scoop with 110 calories, 23 g of protein, 1 g of fat (1 g saturated), 2 g of carbohydrate, 1 g of total sugar with 0 g added, 40 mg of cholesterol and 150 mg of sodium. That is about 74% protein by weight, a solid figure for a flavored powder, though below the 80% or more you see from unflavored isolates. The full ingredient list is whey protein isolate, whey protein hydrolysate, silica, a gum blend (guar, acacia and xanthan), salt, natural flavors, DigeZyme enzymes (protease, amylase, lactase, lipase and cellulase), sucralose, beet juice for color and acesulfame potassium.
          </p>

          <p>
            Munchies was a heavier recipe. The Vitamin Shoppe listing for the discontinued Munchies tub shows 130 calories, 20 g of protein, 7 g of carbs, 2 g of sugar and 300 mg of sodium per scoop, because it mixes in real speculoos-style and chocolate cookie pieces. Those cookie pieces add wheat, soy and a little sugar, which is why the <a href="https://www.alaninu.com/pages/faq" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Alani Nu FAQ</a> says all of its products are gluten-free except Munchies Whey Protein. If you find an old Munchies tub, read that label separately; it is not the same macro profile as Fruity Cereal.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sweeteners matter to a lot of buyers. Every Alani whey label we read uses sucralose, and Fruity Cereal adds acesulfame potassium. Both are approved for use in food in the US, and the amounts in a scoop are small, but people who get headaches or stomach upset from artificial sweeteners, or who simply dislike the aftertaste, should know they are there. The beet juice color in Fruity Cereal is a small plus over synthetic dyes.
          </p>

