        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Alani Nu Protein Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Alani Nu does not claim any third-party certification for its whey protein. We checked the Amazon listing, the retailer copies of the label, the Alani Nu FAQ and the brand's current site, and none of them mention NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Choice, Informed Sport, Clean Label Project or published lab results for heavy metals. The labels we read carry only the standard FDA supplement disclaimer and a milk allergen statement.
          </p>

          <p>
            That does not mean the product is contaminated or unsafe. It means you are relying on the manufacturer's own quality control, as you are with most mainstream protein brands, rather than on an outside lab that checks every lot or audits the plant against a banned-substance list. For most gym-goers that is an acceptable trade. For a college, Olympic or professional athlete in a drug-tested sport, it is not, because only a certified product gives you any protection if a supplement turns out to be contaminated.
          </p>

          <p>
            If testing is your deciding factor, the easy fix is a whey isolate that carries a recognized badge. Dymatize states ISO100 is Informed Choice certified, and it comes in Fruity Pebbles, the closest flavor to Alani's Fruity Cereal. Our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">whey isolate guide</a> lists several certified options, and our explainer on <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> covers what heavy-metal testing does and does not tell you.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">No badge, no claim</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">We only call a product third-party tested when the brand or the certifier's database names the program. Alani Nu names none for its whey, so for drug-tested athletes the answer is to choose a certified isolate instead.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

