        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Alani Protein Powder, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Alani Nu Whey suits a fairly specific buyer: someone who wants a low-calorie whey isolate with a sweet, fun flavor, likes Fruity Cereal, and is not in drug-tested sport. For that person, 23 g of protein in 110 calories at about $1.63 per 25 g is a reasonable deal. It also fits people who find regular concentrate whey heavy on the stomach, since isolate and added lactase usually go down easier, though it is not lactose-free.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you compete under a drug-testing program, because Alani names no certification. Skip it if you avoid sucralose or acesulfame potassium, and look at stevia-sweetened isolates instead. Skip it if you want a brand you can reorder in the same flavor for years, since the powder has already left Alani's own store. And skip the Munchies tub if you need gluten-free, because it contains wheat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many Alani shoppers are women looking for a lighter, better-tasting whey. Protein needs do not change by gender, and you do not need a product marketed to women to get a good one; our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-women/">best protein powder for women</a> guide explains why and lists picks on protein per dollar. If you want a lower-calorie option beyond whey, today's sibling review of <a href="/pb2-protein-powder-review/">PB2 protein powder</a> looks at a peanut-based alternative, and our <a href="/pro-jym-protein-powder-review/">Pro JYM review</a> covers a blend built for slower digestion.
          </p>

          <p>
            A health note: whey is a dairy product and Alani's label lists milk as an allergen. Anyone with a milk allergy should avoid it entirely, and people with lactose intolerance should start with a half scoop. If you have kidney disease, are pregnant or breastfeeding, take prescription medication or are an older adult managing a medical condition, check with your doctor or a registered dietitian before adding a protein supplement. For a general sense of how much you need, see <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein do I need</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying from a third-party seller at a markup</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                With the powder gone from alaninu.com, some Amazon and marketplace listings come from resellers. Check that the seller is Amazon.com or an authorized retailer, look at the best-by date in the listing, and compare against the $44.99 list price we saw. Paying $60 for remaining stock erases the cost advantage that makes Alani worth considering.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the shake and the powder are the same product</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 30g Protein Shake is milk protein concentrate, calcium caseinate and cream, with 160 calories per bottle. The powder is whey isolate and hydrolysate with 110 calories per scoop in Fruity Cereal. They digest differently, cost very differently per gram and carry different ingredients, so a review of one tells you little about the other.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating Munchies as gluten-free</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Alani's own FAQ says every product is gluten-free except Munchies Whey Protein, because the cookie pieces contain wheat. Munchies also contains soy and has more carbs, sugar and sodium than Fruity Cereal. Anyone with celiac disease or a wheat allergy should skip that tub, however good the reviews look.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Calling it lactose-free</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Isolate, hydrolysate and added lactase make Alani whey low in lactose, and many people with mild intolerance handle it well. But Alani does not label it lactose-free, and it still contains milk. If you are very sensitive, start with half a scoop, or choose a product that is explicitly labeled lactose-free or plant-based.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming testing because the brand is popular</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Big social reach and retail shelf space are not the same as a certification. Alani names no NSF, Informed Choice or Informed Sport program for its whey. Tested athletes should pick a product they can find in the certifier's own database, such as Dymatize ISO100, rather than trust a brand's popularity.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

